<BaseLayout title="Letter Tet">
  <article dir="ltr">
    <p class="dim small">2 November 2026</p>
    <h1>Letter Tet</h1>

    <p>
      Tet says: we do not need to be perfect. But when you are building yourself, editing yourself, at the same time you are destroying a part of you. That is where this letter starts.
    </p>

    <h2>The Artist and the Park</h2>
    <p>
      Remember the artist's vision. He went meditating and thinking of a beautiful place, and he saw the vision of a park in his thinking — thinking he will go to the park and see some beautiful things, some trees and animals and maybe some flowers. The artist became tranquilised, and he went to the actual park, and he saw what he manifested: plain, simple and beautiful.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing was missing. The vision and the place were the same thing. He did not need to add anything to it. That is the first part — what was given was already enough.
    </p>

    <h2>Paris, the Heart of the Devil</h2>
    <p>
      Suddenly the devil rose up in his heart. Beautiful garden — why don't I craft it to France, a land of fantasy, Paris, the heart of the devil? So he got his axe and he destroyed the garden. Out of the garden he collected trees, he collected flowers, and out of the garden, as he was hungry, he hunted some wild animals and roasted their flesh to eat. And the artist said: oh, God Almighty is the provider.
    </p>
    <p>
      He had all his flowers and trees, so he said: why not put this into creativity, into artistry, and make something more blunt, perhaps vulgar? The heart of the devil — Paris combined lust and gluttony (greed).
    </p>
    <p>
      See the movement. First he received. Then he wanted more. Then he broke what he had received in order to make something louder out of it. The trees were for building, the flowers for showing, the animals for eating — everything in the garden had to become something else.
    </p>

    <h2>Go Be the Driver</h2>
    <p>
      This is the artificial sense of creation — the freewill which man possesses toward creation itself. God said: oh human, go be the driver. And see what your drive leads you to. Good and bad things, you will follow them. However, think: what I gave you was most precious.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gift was precious before the axe. The drive is yours, and that is the whole risk of it. You can follow the good, and you can follow the bad, and both are on the same road.
    </p>

    <h2>The Old Self We Destroy</h2>
    <p>
      It is the same with us people. When we grow up, we destroy our old self, and that is where insecurities rise up — thinking, oh, how will I look, and what will people think? Well, Tet says: in this unreasonable world, people could be unreasonable some days and some times. So don't be too hard on yourself, and don't be too hard on people.
    </p>
    <p>
      Think: you are corruptible and a sinner like other people. And if they judge, you will judge yourself based on their words — which are as per their perception, which are not true and could be biased. You take their words home with you and repeat them to yourself until they sound like your own voice. But what other people think of you doesn't have to determine what you think of yourself. Their picture of you is not you.
    </p>

    <h2>Before the Labels</h2>
    <p>
      When we are children, such things don't exist. We laugh at each other's features and we roast each other and sometimes we even fight — yet we have fun, and obviously we forgive each other. Nothing sticks. The fight ends and the game starts again, and nobody keeps a record.
    </p>
    <p>
      As human beings grow, their features harden up into the version they want to show other people — to be accepted by society, so that we accidentally do not adhere to any taboo. The face becomes a performance. The child had no performance; the grown person has nothing but.
    </p>
    <p>
      I think in this term, Gary Vaynerchuk says rightly: "stop labeling shit." It has deep resonance. What are these unnecessary words that give us troubles? Who are these high moralists who claim the high ground? As God made everything equal.
    </p>

    <h2>Tet's Two Properties</h2>
    <p>
      Tet has two unique properties. First, it resonates with the Torah verse "God created earth and called it dry land." And secondly, it resonates with hearing. But the hearing here is not the hearing like being able to hear others more, or being vigilant.
    </p>
    <p>
      This hearing, in practice, is to hear yourself — your own existence and your soul — more. Tet is a state of balance. It is kind of a shmuck face here: equally playful and equally righteous and honest. And by righteous I mean not following man-made morals, but following the true from inside, from the pure soul — to listen to that, and to be in your genuine capacity.
    </p>
    <p>
      Playful and righteous at once. Not too hard on yourself, not too soft either. That is the balance — the middle holds both.
    </p>

    <h2>Real-Time Evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Rest: how you deal with the world, and how the world deals with you, is evidence in real time. You don't need anyone's verdict on it. Watch what happens between you and the world, and that is the proof. That is the only record that matters.
    </p>

